# AKS: How to get automation kubeconfig for a serviceaccount.

## Prerequesties

For the sake of illustration, the service account is called `foobar` in the default namespace, which can be created with:

```bash
kubectl create sa foobar
```

Also we need an AKS cluster (name: fbcluster, group: fbgroup)

All commands are assumed to be run in bash.

## Steps


```bash
# Get the service account token from service account secret 
token_secret_name=`kubectl get sa foobar -ojsonpath='{.secrets[0].name}'`
token=`kubectl get secret $token_secret_name -ojsonpath='{.data.token}' | base64 -d`

# Generate a "clean" admin kubeconfig file with azure-cli
az aks get-credentials -n fbcluster -g fbgroup --admin -f out.yaml

# (non-op) view the new kubeconfig file
kubectl --kubeconfig=out.yaml config view

# Remove the admin user and credential
kubectl --kubeconfig=out.yaml unset users.clusterAdmin_fbgroup_fbcluster

# Add credential for the serviceaccount
kubectl --kubeconfig=out.yaml config set-credentials foobar --token="$token"

# Configure context with service account user
kubectl --kubeconfig=out.yaml config set-context --current --user=foobar

# now the out.yaml is able to access cluster as the foobar service account
kubectl --kubeconfig=out.yaml get pod
```

